" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1254x705+0+221/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F0e%2Fb3%2F63435540497d8bce6aba439bae25%2Fscreenshot-2026-09-05-180647.png" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/png" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1254x705+0+221/resize/1100/quality/50/format/png/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F0e%2Fb3%2F63435540497d8bce6aba439bae25%2Fscreenshot-2026-09-05-180647.png" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1254x705+0+221/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F0e%2Fb3%2F63435540497d8bce6aba439bae25%2Fscreenshot-2026-09-05-180647.png" class="img" alt="First-time crop artist Bethany Nagan works on a piece referencing the 1830 &amp;quot;Liberty Leading the People&amp;quot; by Eugène Delacroix and swaps Lady Liberty for a rebel loon on Aug. 19." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 First-time crop artist Bethany Nagan works on a piece referencing the 1830 "Liberty Leading the People" by Eugène Delacroix and swaps Lady Liberty for a rebel loon on Aug. 19.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Alex V. Cipolle/MPR News                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement operation that took place in Minnesota last winter, known as Operation Metro Surge, inspired many of the crop art and fine art submissions at this year's Minnesota State Fair. Artists used wordplay and resistance imagery to process what happened in their state. Crop art has a long tradition of responding to political issues, current events and themes of resistance. This year, Minnesotans submitted a record-breaking 594 pieces of art — 143 more than the previous record set last year. Some contestants made tributes to Renee Macklin Good and Alex Pretti, who were killed by federal agents. Others were inspired by MPR News journalist Sam Stroozas, who rushed out of her home in a robe and slippers to document a car crash involving federal agents. Whistles, which residents used to alert neighbors to the presence of ICE agents, and images of the rebel loon were also on display. See &lt;a href="https://www.mprnews.org/story/2026/08/28/minnesota-state-fair-art-trends-ice-resistance-rebel-loons" target="_blank"&gt;photos of some of the artwork&lt;/a&gt; and read more about this year's Minnesota State Fair. (via &lt;a href="https://www.mprnews.org/" target="_blank"&gt;MPR News&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-142301" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 8256;         --source-height: 4644;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2F9b%2Faa791a9e4cbba38ad4dbb24fefff%2Fgettyimages-2174172163.jpg" class="img" alt="A United Nations flag flies at the United Nations campus in Bonn, western Germany." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A United Nations flag flies at the United Nations campus in Bonn, western Germany.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Ina Fassbender/AFP via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;The United Nations General Assembly has backed a new map that &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/04/nx-s1-5958611/africa-world-map-un-vote" target="_blank"&gt;accurately represents Africa's relative size&lt;/a&gt;, with only the U.S. voting against it.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The Colorado Peaches are recognized as the &lt;a href="https://www.kunc.org/news/2026-09-02/35-years-later-the-oldest-womens-softball-team-in-colorado-is-still-leaning-into-the-game-of-aging" target="_blank"&gt;oldest women's softball team in the country&lt;/a&gt;. Members range in age from their 60s to their 90s. (via &lt;a href="https://www.kunc.org/" target="_blank"&gt;KUNC&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Farmers, chefs and a secretive vegetable broker are &lt;a href="https://www.stlpr.org/health-science-environment/2026-08-31/st-louis-huitlacoche-corn-smut-dealer" target="_blank"&gt;introducing a corn fungus&lt;/a&gt; that is considered a culinary delicacy in Mexico to the people of St. Louis. (via &lt;a href="https://www.stlpr.org/" target="_blank"&gt;STLPR&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/859721331/suzanne-nuyen"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Suzanne Nuyen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
